From: pcm@world.std.com (Peter C. McCluskey)
Date: Mon, 18 Jul 1994 19:11:30 -0400
Subject: [#94-7-194] If Knowledge Limits Learning 

 price@price.demon.co.uk  (Michael Clive Price) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-7-153:

 In the context of single neuron learning, it seems a bit strange for
Robin to call hand-crafted input representations built-in knowledge, since
I normally think of that as guidance from an external teacher. But there
has to be some built-in knowledge in the nervous system, either in the
form of hard-wired connections or non-random initial weights, to explain
the learning patterns we observe. And the fact that we don't know how to
improve on random weights only shows that it is a hard problem.

>Structuring the net means choosing the number of hidden layers, nodes
>per layer etc.  You seem to be talking about brain architectures,
>whereas I am talking about the connection strengths, which are usually
>randomised at start, which seems the more relevant point.  The types of
>knowledge contained in the architecture and the knowledge contained in
>the more dynamic connections are probably completely different and
>inequivalent.  It could be very dangerous to draw an analogy between
>them.

 I am having a hard time imagining any major dangers.

>> Also, it is natural to think of the basic "local learning rules" 
~>> as embodying at least a few knowledge chunks.
>
>No, I don't see this at all.  Since I'm guessing that the same "local
>learning rules" will operate on all scale networks then they contain no
>knowledge about the environment or any species-specific information.

 Based on my experience with neural nets, I don't believe that learning
rules scale up tractably. Backprop-like rules are only usefull at a level
equivalent to a single neuron. I expect that something like Edelman's Neural
Darwinism is needed to explain the next level up (which neurons talk to which).

>> The concept of knowledge chucks was created to help explain cognitive
>> science data on real humans (running on real neural nets).  The idea
>> that people who know more about some area can discover things about
>> it faster is also well supported by data on people.
>
>So why do people learn faster when younger?  I was under the impression
>that learning rates drop with age.  Look at language acquisition, for
>instance.  Back propagation work fastest at the beginning, as well.

 If the human mind were one big backprop net, it would stop any usefull
learning before birth. I think that there are 2 elements to the phenomenon
you note. First, there is something like backprop's learning rate parameter,
which may decrease with age or as a function of the "surprise value" of new
inputs (which should normally diminish with age).
 Second, there is the fact that people try to fit new data into existing
concepts before attempting to create new concepts. I think Robin is concerned
mainly with adding totally new concepts, not the much easier problem of adding
new data that fits within existing concepts.
 There was an talk at Alife 4 by John Batali on "Innate Biases and Critical
Periods: Combining Evolution and Learning in the Acquisition of Syntax".
He showed that much of the language acquisition behavior seen in humans can
be explained by the assumption that the neurons involved in language settle
into a set of weights consistent with one grammar in early childhood. This
makes it harder to learn new grammar types because the networks are "stuck"
in a region corresponding to the 1st grammar. This model shows that
non-random initial weights will limit the possible grammars that a human
can learn (helping to explain why all human languages share some features).
It also explains why children not exposed to language by a certain age
can't learn a language (they learn a grammar that is based on noise).

 kwatson@netcom.com  (Kennita Watson) writes in X-Message-Number: #94-7-149:
>I think the neural net could learn about the world at the same rate as the
>human if and only if it had the same level of feedback, and the same
>ability to affect its environment in ways that are meaningful to it, that
>the human has.

 Existing neural net models either learn what they are told, or learn to
categorize. Humans exhibit learning which does not fit within these rules.

